{
  "schemaVersion": "1.3",
  "kind": "archsim.architecture",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "Video streaming: CDN, origin shield and object storage",
    "description": "Video on demand for a mobile app and for web and smart-TV players, at 1,500 segment requests a second (about 6,000 viewers of 4-second segments). Anycast routing sends each viewer to one of three CDN edge POPs (US East, Europe, Asia Pacific). Each POP reads through: a segment it does not hold comes from a Varnish origin shield in US East, and what the shield does not hold is packaged by a Go packager from a source fragment in S3. Segments never change, so both tiers keep them for a day; how often a request hits is not a fixed ratio but follows from that TTL and from how popular each of 100,000 segment files is (Zipf 1.2), and the caches start empty. Players retry a failed segment. Latency here is time to first byte: download time is not modelled, and the cost leaves out CDN data transfer, the largest line of a real video bill. All numbers are illustrative assumptions.",
    "tags": [
      "cdn",
      "video",
      "varnish",
      "read-through",
      "ttl",
      "object-storage",
      "client-groups"
    ]
  },
